@database "agent_continuation"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: agent_continuation.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Agent Continuation"
@{h1}Agent Continuation

An agent that answers "I will now look at the config file" and stops has
not done its work, but the run ends there all the same: a reply without a
tool call is taken as the final answer. Agent Continuation reads every such
reply of an agent that switched it on and decides whether it is a result
or only a status report. For a status report it has the run add one more
message -- "continue" -- and the agent goes on. The same hook can send a
fixed list of follow-up questions after the agent's answer ("check your
result once more"), and can send an answer back when the agent skipped a
sub-agent it must start. It has no tools and no panel.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  The continuation hook  " link hook}
  @{"  Deciding: rules and judges  " link judges}
  @{"  Follow-ups and required spawns  " link scripted}
  @{"  What stops it  " link limits}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node hook "The continuation hook"
@{h1}The continuation hook

@{tt}evaluate_completion@{utt} runs after every LLM call of an agent that
switched it on, before message_debugger's hooks. It is off by default.

@{h2}When it does something

@{bullet}A reply with tool calls, or with no text, is left alone -- the run
goes on or handles it anyway. (Tool calls are still looked at for
@{" required spawns " link scripted}.)
@{bullet}A text reply is checked in this order: the
@{" budget " link limits} (used up: the reply is final); the
@{" required spawns " link scripted}; the configured
@{" rules or judge " link judges}; when that says final, the next
@{" follow-up " link scripted}, if one is due. Otherwise the reply is the
final answer.

The hook itself changes nothing. It returns @{tt}continue: true@{utt}
with the message to send, and the agent loop does the rest: it appends
that message to the history as a @{tt}user@{utt} message and makes the
next LLM call. The loop does so only while a step is left -- on the
agent's last step (@{tt}max_steps@{utt}) the signal is dropped and the
reply stands as the answer.

@{h2}What it appends

For a status report, unless the agent sets its own
@{tt}continue_message@{utt}, the text is:

@{code text}
Your previous response appears to be an intermediate status update, not a final result.  Continue executing the remaining steps of your task.  Do not repeat status information — proceed with the next action.
@{body}

A follow-up sends its configured text, a missing spawn its configured
message (see @{" Follow-ups and required spawns " link scripted}). Each
message carries an @{tt}injected_by@{utt} mark that says who wrote it:

@{table}
injected_by | The message
agent_continuation | a status report was sent back to work
agent_continuation.followup | a scripted follow-up
agent_continuation.required_spawns | an answer that skipped a required sub-agent
@{body}

The marks are fixed strings, the same whatever the plugin instance is
called. The mark is what tells the rest of the system that nobody typed
the message: the plugin counts its own follow-ups by it, and the context
summarizer keeps the turn open across it
(see @{" The summarizing hook " link context_summarizer/hook}). The mark
is never sent to the model.

The chat shows a status line @{i}Auto-continue #2: keyword_continue
match: 'next I'@{ui} -- the count and the reason.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the agent's YAML; its settings go in the same entry:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      agent_continuation.evaluate_completion:
        enabled: true
        strategy: rules
        default: final
        max_continuations: 5
        rules:
          - type: keyword_continue
            keywords: ["I will now", "next I"]
@{body}

@{h2}Where the settings come from

@{bullet}The agent's override, as above, without @{tt}enabled@{utt},
@{tt}timeout@{utt} and @{tt}order@{utt}. When anything is left, that
entry is the agent's configuration.
@{bullet}Otherwise @{tt}agent_rules.<agent name>@{utt} in the plugin's
server entry. The two are not merged: an override with one setting hides
the agent's @{tt}agent_rules@{utt} entry completely.
@{bullet}A key missing in both takes the plugin-wide value (see
@{" Setting it up " link setup}).

@{table}
Key (per agent) | Default | Meaning
strategy | rules | rules, llm, decision or hybrid; see @{" Deciding " link judges}
default | final | rules: what an answer no rule matched is
rules | none | the rule list; see @{" Deciding " link judges}
continue_message | the text above | what a status report is answered with; empty takes the default
max_continuations | 10 | continuations per request; see @{" What stops it " link limits}
llm_prompt | the plugin's | the llm judge's prompt
hybrid_fallback | llm | hybrid: the judge when no rule matched
decision_profile, decision_question, decision_final_means, decision_continue_means, decision_threshold | the plugin's | the decision judge
followups | none | follow-up messages; see @{" Follow-ups " link scripted}
followups_on_continue | true | false: follow-ups on a session's first request only
required_spawns | none | sub-agents the answer needs; see @{" Required spawns " link scripted}
@{body}

@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} (the llm judge's model) is plugin-wide only.
@endnode

@node judges "Deciding: rules and judges"
@{h1}Deciding: rules and judges

@{table}
strategy | Decides by | Costs
rules | the agent's rules | nothing
llm | a chat model answering FINAL or CONTINUE | one call per text reply
decision | a decision model's probability that the reply is final | one call per text reply
hybrid | the rules; when none matched, hybrid_fallback (llm or decision) | a call only when no rule matched
@{body}

An unknown strategy logs a warning and makes every reply final.

@{h2}Rules

@{table}
type | Settings | Fires when
keyword_continue | keywords, regex | a keyword is in the reply
keyword_final | keywords, regex | a keyword is in the reply
min_length | min_chars (200) | the reply is shorter
step_check | min_steps (3) | the reply comes in the request's first min_steps LLM calls
@{body}

Keywords match anywhere in the reply, ignoring case; with
@{tt}regex: true@{utt} they are regular expressions (an invalid one is
logged and skipped). The keywords of all rules are collected first; when a
continue and a final keyword both match, continue wins. A
@{tt}min_length@{utt} or @{tt}step_check@{utt} rule that fires means
continue, whatever the keywords say. When nothing matched,
@{tt}default@{utt} decides -- except under hybrid, where the judge does.

@{h2}The llm judge

The model of the plugin-wide @{tt}llm_profile@{utt} gets
@{tt}llm_prompt@{utt} as its only message, with @{tt}{{ agent_name }}@{utt}
and @{tt}{{ response }}@{utt} replaced (written exactly so, with the
spaces) -- the reply cut at 3000 characters. An answer containing
@{tt}CONTINUE@{utt} anywhere continues; anything else is final. The
client is made on first use and kept until a restart.

@{h2}The decision judge

A decision model (@{tt}decision_profile@{utt}, empty: the system's
default decision profile) is asked @{tt}decision_question@{utt} about
the agent's name and the reply (cut at 3000 characters), with
@{tt}decision_final_means@{utt} and @{tt}decision_continue_means@{utt}
as the meaning of yes and no -- both, or neither when one is empty. It
answers the probability that the reply is final; the run continues while
that is below @{tt}decision_threshold@{utt} (0.5). A threshold outside
0.0 to 1.0 -- 70 meant as a percent -- is refused with a warning: in an
agent's entry the plugin-wide value is used, in the plugin's own entry 0.5. The probability is in the log and in the
status line's reason: @{i}decision model: p(final)=0.03 < 0.5@{ui}.

@{h2}When a judge fails

No profile, no prompt or question, an error or a timeout: the reply is
final. Stopping is the safe mistake; a judge that cannot answer must not
keep a loop running.
@endnode

@node scripted "Follow-ups and required spawns"
@{h1}Follow-ups and required spawns

@{h2}Follow-ups

Agents often find their own mistakes when asked to look again.
@{tt}followups@{utt} is a list of messages sent one per final answer, in
order:

@{code yaml}
agent_continuation.evaluate_completion:
  enabled: true
  followups:
    - "Check your result against the source once more. Fix what is wrong."
    - "Now give the complete final result again, in the required format."
@{body}

@{bullet}A follow-up comes only after an answer the strategy calls final;
a status report gets the continue message first.
@{bullet}The caller gets the last answer only. If the agent has an output
format, the last follow-up must ask for the whole result again.
@{bullet}The plugin keeps no list of what it sent: it counts the messages
marked @{tt}agent_continuation.followup@{utt} since the message that
opened the request (what a person or a caller wrote, or the wake of a
woken run). A new request starts the list again. A single text written
without the list dash counts as one follow-up.
@{bullet}@{tt}followups_on_continue: false@{utt} sends them only on a
session's first request: when an assistant answer comes before the
request's opening message, the request continues an earlier one and gets
none. Only true or false count; anything else logs a warning and keeps
the follow-ups on.

@{h2}Required spawns

Some agents must hand their work to a sub-agent before they answer.
@{tt}required_spawns@{utt} checks it before any strategy runs, and sends
an answer back when a required sub-agent was never started:

@{code yaml}
agent_continuation.evaluate_completion:
  enabled: true
  max_continuations: 2
  required_spawns:
    agents: "reviewers if review_enabled else []"
    tool: my_sam_manage_sub_agent
    message: "The review is missing: {missing} was never started."
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Meaning
agents | a Jinja expression over the agent's @{tt}template_vars@{utt} (the session's on top) that gives a list of agent types, or one
tool | the sub-agent manager tool the agent starts them with
message | the text sent back; @{tt}{missing}@{utt} is replaced by the missing types. Without it: "Required sub-agents were never spawned: {missing}. Spawn them and finish the step they belong to."
@{body}

@{bullet}Started means a @{tt}create@{utt} through that tool (or a call
with @{tt}agent_type@{utt} and no operation) whose result names an
instance and did not end in @{tt}error@{utt} or @{tt}cancelled@{utt}.
@{bullet}The hook remembers every create it sees, per session, until an
answer of that session is final or its budget runs out. A create that pruning later took out of
the history still counts.
@{bullet}Missing @{tt}agents@{utt} or @{tt}tool@{utt}, an expression that
fails, or one that gives no list: the check is off for that answer, with a
warning. It can cost a continuation, never block an answer.
@endnode

@node limits "What stops it"
@{h1}What stops it

@{bullet}@{b}The budget@{ub}: @{tt}max_continuations@{utt} (10) messages
per request, counting all three kinds together. At the limit the reply is
final and no follow-up is sent. The agent's own value wins over the
plugin's. A value that is not a whole number of at least 1 (0, a word,
@{tt}true@{utt}, 2.5, @{tt}.inf@{utt}) is refused with a warning -- in the agent's entry the
plugin's value is used, in the plugin's entry 10.
@{bullet}@{b}The steps@{ub}: every continuation is one more LLM call of
the agent's @{tt}max_steps@{utt}. On the last step the loop ignores the
signal and ends with the reply. Follow-ups need steps left for them.
@{bullet}@{b}A final answer@{ub} clears the count of the request, and so
does reaching the budget. If the run goes on after that -- a person wrote
into it -- the budget starts again.
@{bullet}@{b}Failures@{ub}: a judge that fails, and a hook that runs past
its 30 seconds, leave the reply final.

The counts live in the server process, at most 10000 requests and as many
sessions; a request that continues moves to the newest place, so the
oldest -- mostly runs that ended without a final answer -- go first.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

After a reply the hook sends back, the model sees one more message from
the user: the continue message, a follow-up or the spawn message, exactly
as configured. Nothing marks it as not typed by a person -- the mark stays
inside the system -- so write these texts as a person would say them.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The message is appended at the end of the history; nothing earlier is
rewritten, so the provider's prompt cache still covers everything before
it. Each continuation costs one more call of the agent's model with the
whole history. The llm and decision judges cost a call of their own
model per text reply they judge (hybrid: only when no rule matched); they
do not touch the agent's history or its cache. The messages stay in the
session like any other.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Follow-ups are counted from the history. When the context
summarizer or pruning has removed the marked messages, a follow-up is sent
again, and a continued request can look like a session's first one to
@{tt}followups_on_continue@{utt}. The budget is the ceiling.
@{bullet}The llm and decision judges read only the first 3000 characters
of a reply; a status line at the end of a long one is not seen.
@{bullet}The plugin cannot reload its settings: a change in its server
entry takes effect after a restart, and so do the judges' clients.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt});
the hook still runs only for agents that switch it on:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    agent_continuation:
      type: agent_continuation
      enabled: true
      config:
        max_continuations: 10
        strategy: "rules"
        llm_profile: "turbo"
        default_continue_message: >
          Your previous response appears to be an intermediate status update,
          not a final result.  Continue executing the remaining steps of your
          task.  Do not repeat status information — proceed with the next
          action.
@{body}

No tool, so no allowlist line. No extra packages.

@{h2}Server settings

These go under @{tt}config:@{utt}; what is not set there takes the default
below. They apply to every agent that has not set the key itself.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_continuations | 10 | continuations per request
default_continue_message | the text in @{" The continuation hook " link hook} | what a status report is answered with
strategy | rules | rules, llm, decision or hybrid
llm_profile | turbo | the llm judge's model
llm_prompt | see schema.yaml | the llm judge's prompt
hybrid_fallback | llm | hybrid's judge when no rule matched
decision_profile | empty: the default decision profile | the decision judge's model
decision_question | Is this the agent's FINAL answer to its task? | what the decision judge is asked
decision_final_means | A finished result: ... | what yes means
decision_continue_means | An intermediate status report, ... | what no means
decision_threshold | 0.5 | continue while p(final) is below it
agent_rules | none | per agent name, the same keys an agent's override takes
@{body}

The plugin stores nothing on disk.
@endnode
